DECORATOR

Problem:
Need to add behavior or responsibilities to an object dynamically, without changing its class.
to prevent separate class for combination
like combination of (compressing,encrypting,... om Image)

Core idea:
Wrap the original object inside another object that has the same interface and adds extra behavior before or after delegating to the original object.
it's like create horizontal chain,
each step add something and pass to the next step

Roles:
Component: common interface                                      (to use polymorphism)
ConcreteComponent: the original object                           (the main logic)
Decorator: base wrapper class implementing the same interface
ConcreteDecorator: adds specific behavior

When to use:
When you want flexible, runtime extension of behavior instead of creating many subclasses.

When not to use:
When the design becomes too layered and hard to understand, or when simple inheritance is enough.

----------------------------------------------------
FACADE

Problem:
A subsystem is complex and hard to use directly.
when we want to encapsulate some separate classes as one functionalities.
like SendNotification class

Core idea:
Provide a simple, unified interface to a set of interfaces in the subsystem.

Roles:
Facade: simplified entry point
Subsystem classes: the underlying complex classes
Client: uses the facade instead of dealing with subsystem details

When to use:
When you want to simplify usage of a complex system or reduce coupling between client and subsystem.

When not to use:
When clients need full control over subsystem details, or when the facade becomes a god object that does too much.

